<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Bahawalpur State</h1><p class="st-meta">1748 CE – 1955 CE (approx.) · capital Bahawalpur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Abbasi Nawabs' desert kingdom along the Sutlej, the largest princely state to join Pakistan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daudpota">Daudpota Abbasi</a> clan, who claimed descent from the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>, moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a> valley in the early 18th century. Around 1748 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahawal-khan-i">Bahawal Khan I</a> founded the town of Bahawalpur, and the Nawabs became independent of the declining <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Nawabs signed a treaty with the British in 1833 and supported them in the Afghan and Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> wars and in 1857. Much of the state was Cholistan desert, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a> Valley Project of the 1920s greatly expanded irrigated farming. The Nawabs built palaces such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noor-mahal">Noor Mahal</a> and held the old fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derawar-fort">Derawar</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadiq-muhammad-khan-v">Sadiq Muhammad Khan V</a> acceded to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Pakistan</a> in October 1947 and gave the new state generous financial support. Bahawalpur was merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-pakistan">West Pakistan</a> in 1955 and is now a division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> province.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bahawalpur-state.jpg" alt="Bahawalpur State" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Icey · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bahawalpur.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1748 CE – 1955 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bahawalpur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bahawal Khan I, Sadiq Muhammad Khan V</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Saraiki</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bahawalpur</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1852&amp;focus=bahawalpur-state" aria-label="Bahawalpur State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6547 1103 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bahawalpur State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6867 1359L6757 1183M6867 1359L7364 1601M6867 1359L7364 1602M6867 1359L6778 1519"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6757 1183h0M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M6778 1519h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6867 1359h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6867 1359h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bahawalpur. <a href="/atlas?year=1852&amp;focus=bahawalpur-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
